@meta
id: 14-18
title: Arithmetic on the match
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-expression
par: 50
minutes: 6
vscode: six lines, a calculator, six edits
open: retry.policy.ts

@brief
Load testing showed the retry delays are too aggressive. Every
`delayMs` in this policy doubles. The `attempts` and `timeoutMs`
values on the same lines do not change.

Six values, all different, each one a function of what is there
already. No literal replacement can express "twice this", and `&`
only gives you the match unchanged. There is a third kind of
replacement: one that begins with `\=` and is evaluated as an
expression, with the match available inside it.

Aim the pattern so that only the number is the match. Chapter 13's
`\zs` is the tool for that.

@keys
| `\=expr` | the replacement is an expression, not text |
| `submatch(0)` | inside the expression: the whole match, as a string |
| `submatch(1)` | group one — the expression form of `\1` |
| `\zs` | start the match here; what came before is context only |

@hints
Match `delayMs: ` for context, but start the actual match at the
digits. Then the replacement can treat the match as a number.
--
`\=submatch(0)*2`. Vim converts the matched digits to a number,
doubles it, and writes the result back where the digits were.
--
`:%s/\vdelayMs: \zs\d+/\=submatch(0)*2/` and Enter.

@solution
    :%s/\vdelayMs: \zs\d+/\=submatch(0)*2/<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Forty-three keystrokes for six calculations you did not do.

`\=` at the start of the replacement changes what the rest of it is.
Instead of text with a few special characters, it is a vim expression,
evaluated once per match, and its result is what gets written.
`submatch(0)` is the whole match and `submatch(1)` onwards are the
groups; they are strings, and vim's arithmetic coerces `"250"` to
`250` when you multiply it. String concatenation is `.`, so
`\=submatch(0) . 'ms'` would append a unit instead.

The pattern is doing as much work as the expression. `delayMs: ` is
there to choose the right number on each line; `\zs` then says the
match proper begins after it, so the expression sees only `250` and
the replacement overwrites only `250`. Without `\zs`, you would be
writing `\='delayMs: ' . submatch(1)*2` and capturing the digits as a
group — legal, and twice as long.

This is the lesson where the substitute stops being a text tool.
Anything you can compute in an expression, you can write into a file
at every match: a doubled value, a padded number, a lower-cased key.
The next lesson uses the one piece of context an expression has that
the pattern does not: where in the file the match is.

@start retry.policy.ts
export const RETRY_POLICY = {
  paymentCapture: { attempts: 3, delayMs: 250, timeoutMs: 30000 },
  paymentRefund: { attempts: 5, delayMs: 500, timeoutMs: 30000 },
  emailSend: { attempts: 3, delayMs: 1000, timeoutMs: 10000 },
  webhookDeliver: { attempts: 8, delayMs: 2000, timeoutMs: 15000 },
  ledgerPost: { attempts: 3, delayMs: 100, timeoutMs: 5000 },
  payoutExport: { attempts: 2, delayMs: 1500, timeoutMs: 60000 },
};

@expected retry.policy.ts
export const RETRY_POLICY = {
  paymentCapture: { attempts: 3, delayMs: 500, timeoutMs: 30000 },
  paymentRefund: { attempts: 5, delayMs: 1000, timeoutMs: 30000 },
  emailSend: { attempts: 3, delayMs: 2000, timeoutMs: 10000 },
  webhookDeliver: { attempts: 8, delayMs: 4000, timeoutMs: 15000 },
  ledgerPost: { attempts: 3, delayMs: 200, timeoutMs: 5000 },
  payoutExport: { attempts: 2, delayMs: 3000, timeoutMs: 60000 },
};
